course: dictionary
lesson: 11062
title: Weepy: tired tears and sad films
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words describe a feeling, and some describe a thing that makes people feel it. «She's been weepy all day because she's overtired.» Weepy means ready to cry, or crying easily. The clue is overtired: when you are very tired, tears can come quickly.
explain: Weepy can also describe a film, book or song. «We watched a weepy film, and Dad pretended he had something in his eye.» The clue is watched a film, and the joke about the eye tells you the film was sad enough to make people cry.
explain: Now read this sentence from a health leaflet: «Crusty, weepy patches need a GP.» Here weepy is not about crying at all. The sentence tells you the patches need a GP, which is a doctor, so context shows that this is a different use. Always check the words around the word, then use a dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: Weepy is an adjective: weepy, weepier, weepiest.
fact: «Weepy» means ready to cry, or crying easily, often because you are tired or sad.
fact: «A weepy» is an informal noun meaning a sad film or story that makes people cry.
fact: Informal means it is a friendly, everyday word, the kind people use at home, not in formal writing.
exercise: «She's weepy because she's overtired.» What does weepy mean here? | Very cheerful and full of jokes | Feeling sick after dinner | Loud and angry | *Ready to cry, or crying easily
exercise: Ella says, «That film was a real weepy.» What is a weepy? | A kind of wet weather | A small brass instrument | A children's game played in the park | *A sad film or story that makes people cry
exercise: In «Crusty, weepy patches need a GP», which word tells you who should look at the patches? | weepy | *GP | crusty | patches
test: «He felt weepy after his team lost the final.» Which meaning fits best? | *Ready to cry, feeling sad | Full of energy and running fast | Covered in mud and rain | Very rich and tasty
test: Mia asks, «Is weepy a formal word?» What is the best answer? | Yes, it is only used in school exams | No, it is only used for describing weather | *No, it is informal, the kind of word used in everyday family talk | Yes, it is used only by doctors
exam: «The weepy film made Grandma cry, and she called it a real tear-jerker.» What does weepy suggest here? | *Sad, and likely to make people cry | Cold and wet | Very loud and noisy | Happy and funny
